    ],
    "proof_tex": "The score index set is nonempty because \\(T\\ge3\\), \\(m\\le\\delta T\\), and \\(\\delta<1\\). Since \\(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}\\) is positive definite, \\(D:=\\|Ke\\|_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}^{-1}}=\\|\\mathbf1_O\\|_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}^{-1}}>0\\). Adding an arbitrary multiple of \\(e\\) changes every coordinate of the calendar path by the same constant and leaves the cohort deviations unchanged. It therefore preserves the Holder restriction, the deviation bound, and calendar centering. Hence \\(e\\) is a two-sided recession direction of the difference class \\(2\\mathcal M_{T,m}(\\beta,L,B,q)\\), with \\(\\ell(e)=1\\).\n\nIf a maximizer \\(h_r\\) had \\(\\|Kh_r\\|_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}^{-1}}<r\\), then \\(h_r+ce\\) would remain feasible for all sufficiently small \\(c>0\\), by the triangle inequality, while \\(\\ell(h_r+ce)=\\ell(h_r)+c\\); this contradicts maximality. Thus the constraint is active. Similarly, for \\(r_2>r_1>0\\), the vector\n\\[\nh_{r_1}+\\frac{r_2-r_1}{D}e\n\\]\nbelongs to the difference class and is feasible at radius \\(r_2\\). Consequently\n\\[\n\\omega_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(r_2)-\n\\omega_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(r_1)\n\\ge\\frac{r_2-r_1}{D}.\n\\]\nTaking the right derivative and using the established concavity and supergradient property of the modulus yields \\(\\omega'_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(r)\\ge D^{-1}>0\\). Thus \\(r\\in\\mathcal R_+\\). The supergradient inequality at \\(r\\), evaluated at zero, is\n\\[\n\\omega_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(0)\n\\le\\omega_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(r)-r\\omega'_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(r),\n\\]\nwhich proves the two upper bounds on \\(s_r\\). The definitions of \\(s_1\\) and \\(b_1=\\{\\omega_{\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{nest}}}(1)-s_1\\}/2\\) give the final assertions.",
